      "description": "The wardens want every tower quiet, while the tide clerks believe the impossible pattern must be recorded and answered before the fog returns. The Bronze Compact is caught between protecting its craft and proving the sound did not come from one of its furnaces.",
      "faction_ids": [
        "fac_parishwardens",
        "fac_tideclerks",
        "fac_bronze_compact"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cnf_missing_ninth",
      "name": "The Empty Frame",
      "description": "The sealed ninth place in Stormward Tower may explain the pattern, but opening it would violate parish law and expose the records that were altered after the Ninefold Silence.",
      "faction_ids": [
        "fac_parishwardens",
        "fac_tideclerks"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "world_id": "wld_ninth_bell_estuary",
  "name": "The Brineward Reach",
  "description": "Brineward is a gaslamp city built on the shifting mudflats of a drowned estuary. Gas mantles and oil lamps stain its fog with small, wavering halos; beyond them, streets vanish into tidewater and the sound of bells travels farther than sight. The city measures weather, curfew, and communal memory by its storm-bells, whose bronze voices are governed by old parish custom. When a bell-founder’s apprentice hears an impossible pattern from the towers, the parish orders every bell silenced before the next fog. Yet the fog has a habit of returning sounds with details no living witness recalls. In Brineward, a chime can be warning, testimony, or an accusation that has waited years to be heard.",
  "locations": [
    {
      "id": "loc_cinderwharf",
      "name": "Cinderwharf",
      "description": "A crowded landing of tarred pilings and chain ferries, where fishmongers work beneath hooded oil lamps. The mud below the wharf preserves footprints until the tide takes them, making it a favored place to trade in secrets and lost cargo."
    },
    {
      "id": "loc_founders_row",
      "name": "Founders' Row",
      "description": "A narrow lane of bronze shops, soot-dark windows, and furnace yards. Apprentices learn to judge a casting by the way cooling metal sighs; the oldest molds bear tiny marks no current guild member claims to recognize."
    },
    {
      "id": "loc_stormward_tower",
      "name": "Stormward Tower",
      "description": "The tallest bell tower stands on a stone island above the flood line. Its nine storm-bells are rung by hand, each assigned to a different danger, though the sealed ninth frame has stood empty since the city’s founding."
    },
    {
      "id": "loc_drowned_parish",
      "name": "The Drowned Parish",
      "description": "A sunken church whose upper gallery emerges only at the lowest tides. Parish records are kept in oilskin bundles on a dry loft, while the submerged nave seems to carry distant chimes even when the towers are still."
    },
    {
      "id": "loc_lantern_archive",
      "name": "Lantern Archive",
      "description": "A compact civic archive in a former customs house, lit by gas mantles and guarded against damp with cedar shelves. Its tide ledgers include notes on bell patterns that were struck out by successive clerks."
    }
  ],
  "factions": [
    {
      "id": "fac_parishwardens",
      "name": "The Parish Wardens",
      "description": "Keepers of the storm-bells and the city’s curfew rites. They insist that an unlicensed chime can summon panic, and have ordered the towers quiet until the next fog passes.",
      "location_ids": [
        "loc_stormward_tower",
        "loc_drowned_parish"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "fac_bronze_compact",
      "name": "The Bronze Compact",
      "description": "Founders and furnace workers who safeguard casting methods as family property. They want the apprentice’s strange pattern examined, but fear the parish will blame their guild for a bell none of them made.",
      "location_ids": [
        "loc_founders_row",
        "loc_cinderwharf"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "fac_tideclerks",
      "name": "The Tide Clerks",
      "description": "Civic record keepers who compare flood marks, fog dates, and public chimes. Their quiet search through altered ledgers has uncovered a pattern that seems to precede events rather than announce them.",
      "location_ids": [
